            <header class="lesson-header">
                <span class="lesson-number">Lesson 1</span>
                <h1>Introduction to Asynchronous Programming</h1>
                <div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>30 min</span><span class="lesson-level intermediate">Intermediate</span></div>
            </header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Why Asynchronous Programming?</h2>
                    <p>Asynchronous programming allows your application to remain responsive while waiting for I/O operations like network requests, file access, or database queries.</p>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Key Insight</span></div>
                        <p>Async doesn't make things faster—it makes better use of resources while waiting.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Synchronous vs Asynchronous</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Synchronous - blocks the thread while waiting
public string GetData()
{
    var data = httpClient.GetStringAsync(url).Result;  // Thread blocked!
    return data;
}

// Asynchronous - frees the thread while waiting
public async Task&lt;string&gt; GetDataAsync()
{
    var data = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url);  // Thread free to do other work
    return data;
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Async vs Parallel</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Asynchronous</h4>
                            <p>About waiting efficiently</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>I/O-bound operations</li>
                                <li>Network requests</li>
                                <li>File/database access</li>
                                <li>Non-blocking waits</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Parallel</h4>
                            <p>About doing multiple things</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>CPU-bound operations</li>
                                <li>Data processing</li>
                                <li>Calculations</li>
                                <li>Multiple cores</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Thread Pool</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// When you await, the thread returns to the pool
// When the operation completes, a thread continues execution
public async Task ProcessAsync()
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Before: Thread {Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId}");
    
    await Task.Delay(100);  // Thread returned to pool during delay
    
    Console.WriteLine($"After: Thread {Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId}");
    // May be a different thread!
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Async is for I/O-bound operations (waiting).</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Parallel is for CPU-bound operations (computing).</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Async frees threads to do other work while waiting.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Never block on async code with .Result or .Wait().</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
